        output='     1\t# Reflections on the Stages of Buddhist Practice\n     2
\t\n     3\t------Delivered at the Symposium on the Stages of Practice in Chines
e Buddhism, Singapore, 2005\n     4\t\n     5\tMaster Jiqun\n     6\t\n     7\tR
eflections on the Stages of Buddhist Practice\n     8\t\n     9\tI. The Core Ele
ments of the Dharma\n    10\t\n    11\t1. Practicing the Path of Liberation\n   
12\t\n    13\t2. Practicing the Bodhisattva Path\n    14\t\n    15\tII. The Stag
es of Practice in the Dharma\n    16\t\n    17\tIII. Putting the Five Elements i
nto Practice\n    18\t\n    19\t1. Taking Refuge\n    20\t\n    21\t2. Generatin
g Aspiration\n    22\t\n    23\t3. Observing Precepts\n    24\t\n    25\t4. Esta
blishing Right View\n    26\t\n    27\t5. Practicing Samatha-Vipassana\n    28\t
\n    29\tIV. Conclusion\n    30\t\n    31\tThe theme of this symposium is "the 
stages of practice in Chinese Buddhism," an issue the Buddhist community urgentl
y needs to clarify today. After years of spreading the Dharma, I have come to fe
el deeply that many people have studied Buddhism for a long time yet remain utte
rly confused about the basic framework of practice. Why is this? Precisely becau
se they have not grasped the essentials or understood the stages.\n    32\t\n   
33\tBuddhism has countless schools and an enormous canon. Among the ten thousand
and more volumes of the Tripitaka, what is essential, and what are the guiding p
rinciples? People either fail to understand or cling to one thing while discardi
ng another; those who truly grasp it all are one in a hundred. No wonder blind, 
reckless practice is so common. There are also people who have gained realizatio
ns through practices such as Buddha-name recitation or Chan meditation, yet, lac
king a systematic understanding of the Dharma, remain stuck at some level, unabl
e to go deeper. It must be understood that a certain meditation experience, or a
slight ease in reciting the Buddha\'s name, is still worlds away from buddhahood
. How do we cut off afflictions? How do we realize emptiness? How do we perfect 
the qualities the Buddhas and bodhisattvas possess? These are all fortresses tha
t must be taken one by one on the path of practice. So where should we begin?\n 
  34\t\n    35\tFirst, we should recognize which core elements a complete system
of study and practice must contain. In other words, grasping these elements mean
s grasping the essentials of the Dharma and the direction of practice. Otherwise
, the constant talk of eighty-four thousand Dharma gates leaves beginners at a l
oss and daunted.\n    36\t\n    37\tSecond, we need to understand what sequence 
of stages the study and practice of Buddhism actually follows. Otherwise, even i
f we learn some theories, we cannot use them effectively when we do not know the
ir place in the whole process of practice or the progressive relations among the
various links of practice.\n    38\t\n    39\tBeyond that, we should be clear ab
out how each step is to be applied in practice. Without mastering the concrete m
ethods, even with the first two conditions in place, we will inevitably end up w
ith empty talk and dogma.\n    40\t\n    41\tI feel that the vagueness and absen
ce of the core elements of the Dharma, the stages of practice, and the practical
methods are precisely the key to why most practitioners today make little progre
ss. Of course, exploring these questions is far from easy. The Dharma is vast: b
eyond the three great traditions of Theravada, Chinese, and Tibetan Buddhism, ea
ch tradition has numerous schools and branches, and each school has its own rela
tively independent structure of study and practice. To set up yet another practi
ce method or yet another sequence would, at best, add one more to the eighty-fou
r thousand Dharma gates---making eighty-four thousand and one gates and one extr
a sequence---which would hardly have much practical meaning.\n    42\t\n    43\t
For this reason, the core elements and stages of practice we need to explore sho
uld not conflict with any school and should apply to the study and practice of e
very method. Just as a computer\'s operating system must be highly compatible in
order to run all kinds of software and support all kinds of work, so---if differ
ent schools and practice methods are compared to software---the core elements, s
tages of practice, and practical methods of the Dharma are like the operating sy
stem of a computer. If the system itself has flaws, it will not only affect the 
running of software but bring down the entire system. Therefore, building a soun
d, practical system is the lifeline of whether Buddhism can develop healthily, a
nd the mission we Buddhists of this generation cannot shirk.\n    44\t\n    45\t
{#一佛法的核心要素}\n    46\t## I. The Core Elements of the Dharma\n    47\t\n  
 48\tAll the major language traditions and schools of Buddhism have their own re
latively independent structures of study and practice, but however much they var
y, they still share common core elements. Only by grasping these cores can we be
tter understand the practice of each school. So what are the core elements of th
e Dharma? I think they are precisely taking refuge, generating aspiration, obser
ving precepts, establishing right view, and practicing samatha-vipassana.\n    4
9\t\n    50\tLet us consider: which method of practice can do without these five
? Perhaps someone will ask: why these and not others? Why not four or six? There
are two reasons. First, as core elements, they must encompass all of Buddhism. S
econd, as core elements, they must apply to all schools without being too many i
n number---otherwise they would no longer be core.\n    51\t\n    52\tWhy can th
ese five elements encompass all of Buddhism? The Dharma distinguishes the path o
f liberation from the bodhisattva path; every school and method belongs to one o
r the other. The goal of the path of liberation is to develop undefiled wisdom, 
realize emptiness, attain nirvana, and accomplish liberation. The goal of the bo
dhisattva path is to perfect the qualities of the Buddha\'s wisdom and compassio
n, thereby freeing ourselves from delusion and karma and attaining great freedom
. These goals in turn lock in the necessary conditions for reaching them.\n    5
3\t\n    54\t{#解脱道的修行}\n    55\t### 1. Practicing the Path of Liberation\n
   56\t\n    57\tFor the practice of the path of liberation, first of all, we sh
ould have full confidence in its goal and methods---believing that liberation is
of great significance to life and that practicing in accordance with the Dharma 
will surely bring liberation. Without such confidence, how can practice even be 
spoken of? Taking refuge is precisely arousing confidence in the goal of liberat
ion (the Buddha), the methods of the path of liberation (the Dharma), and the te
achers who guide us toward liberation (the Sangha), and, through a ceremony cond
ucted in accordance with the Dharma, establishing a close relationship of trust 
and reliance with them and surrendering to them wholeheartedly. When we make the
vow before the Buddhas and bodhisattvas---"For my entire life, I take refuge in 
the Buddha; for my entire life, I take refuge in the Dharma; for my entire life,
I take refuge in the Sangha"---it means this relationship is established.\n    5
8\t\n    59\tBut faith alone is not enough; we must go further and give rise to 
the wish to seek it---that is, arouse renunciation. The lives of ordinary beings
either drift with the deluded stream or are swayed by circumstances---blind, una
ble to be their own masters. The arising of renunciation represents a conscious 
choice of life. It is not only the beginning of practice on the path of liberati
on but runs through the entire process of practice; only with liberation is renu
nciation brought to full completion.\n    60\t\n    61\tSo what is it that binds
us, keeping us from freedom and liberation? It is delusion and karma---the delud
ed karmic stream accumulated over countless lives. Observing the precepts is pre
cisely the key to stopping this stream. Precepts have the meaning of preventing 
wrong and stopping evil---that is, ceasing unwholesome deeds such as killing, st
ealing, sexual misconduct, and false speech. By regulating our own conduct, we s
ubdue afflictions and stop the continuation of the deluded stream.\n    62\t\n  
 63\tBut stopping is not the same as eradicating. In fact, the force of the delu
ded stream remains; the afflictions of greed, hatred, and ignorance remain, only
temporarily dormant because they get no chance to act. Right view is the true we
apon for cutting off delusion and karma. Right view is of two kinds: worldly rig
ht view and transcendent right view. Through hearing and contemplating the scrip
tures, we can gain worldly right view and the ability to distinguish the true fr
om the false and the right from the wrong. Only when we know what is true and fa
lse, what is right and wrong, can we adopt and reject appropriately in the cours
e of practice, cut off delusion, and realize truth.\n    64\t\n    65\tHowever, 
the right view gained through hearing and contemplation is intellectual and conc
eptual; set against the stream of delusion flowing since beginningless time, its
force is still no match. This is why we must rely on samatha-vipassana to bring 
the right view of hearing and contemplation into mind and conduct, realize empti
ness, and give rise to undefiled right view. Only then can we cut off the delude
d stream, completely eradicate the root of delusion and karma, attain nirvana, a
nd accomplish liberation.\n    66\t\n    67\t{#菩萨道的修行}\n    68\t### 2. Pra
cticing the Bodhisattva Path\n    69\t\n    70\tThe practice of the bodhisattva 
path likewise requires confidence in the fruit of buddhahood and the methods of 
the bodhisattva path---believing that accomplishing the Buddha\'s qualities has 
unsurpassable value for life, and also believing that practicing the bodhisattva
path will surely help us accomplish these qualities. This is the conviction ever
y bodhisattva practitioner must possess. Taking refuge is arousing confidence in
the goal of the bodhisattva path (the Buddha), the methods of practicing it (the
Dharma), and the teachers who can guide us toward supreme enlightenment (the San
gha), and, through a ceremony conducted in accordance with the Dharma, surrender
ing body and mind in sincere reliance.\n    71\t\n    72\tAdmiring the Buddha\'s
sublime qualities and believing in the greatness of the bodhisattva spirit, we y
earn toward it and arouse an earnest bodhicitta. As the Yogacarabhumi Sastra def
ines it, this mind is the wish that one "will certainly attain unexcelled, compl
ete, perfect enlightenment" and "will be able to accomplish every benefit of sen
tient beings." Bodhicitta contains the meanings of self-awakening and benefiting
others---precisely the mental foundation on which the Buddha accomplishes the tw
o great qualities of compassion and wisdom: self-awakening comes from wisdom, an
d benefiting others comes from compassion.\n    73\t\n    74\tIn short, practice
on the bodhisattva path comes down to two great tasks: abandoning the ordinary m
ind and accomplishing the Buddha\'s qualities. How should a bodhisattva who has 
just generated the aspiration set about this? This requires receiving the bodhis
attva precepts and practicing the bodhisattva deeds. The content of the bodhisat
tva deeds is none other than the six perfections, which are divided into two par
ts: skillful means and wisdom. The first five perfections are skillful means, th
rough which we accomplish the Buddha\'s quality of compassion; the sixth is wisd
om, aroused by establishing right view through hearing and contemplating the Mah
ayana scriptures. The Lamrim (the Stages of the Path to Enlightenment) says: "If
one learns only one of the two---skillful means or wisdom---one cannot attain bu
ddhahood." This shows that only by practicing both skillful means and wisdom can
we perfectly accomplish the two qualities of compassion and wisdom with which th
e Buddha is endowed. If we neglect the cultivation of compassion, we will fall i
nto the two lesser vehicles; and without accomplishing right view, we cannot aba
ndon the ordinary mind.\n    75\t\n    76\tThe cultivation of compassion and wis
dom both depend on samatha-vipassana. Through contemplative practice such as Sha
ntideva\'s "exchanging self and other" or the Venerable Atisha\'s "seven causes 
and effects," we can cultivate the quality of compassion; through contemplating 
the right view of Consciousness-only or Madhyamaka, we can accomplish undefiled 
wisdom. Practice on the bodhisattva path is precisely the continual nurturing of
compassion and wisdom through samatha-vipassana, ultimately perfecting the Buddh
a\'s qualities.\n    77\t\n    78\tFrom this it is clear that, whether on the pa
th of liberation or the bodhisattva path, the five elements are indispensable; t
he lack of any one of them will become an obstacle to practice and even affect t
he healthy development of Buddhism. Clarifying these cores, we can understand th
e basic content a complete system of study and practice should contain, and so e
xamine whether our own practice is complete and how it should be supplemented an
d improved. At the same time, it helps us simplify the complex and go straight t
o the essentials, so that on the path of practice we are not dazzled by a riot o
f colors, unable to tell the primary from the secondary. It can also resolve the
one-sidedness that often appears in practice---treating precepts as everything o
r samatha-vipassana as everything, clinging to one and losing sight of the Dharm
a.\n    79\t\n    80\t{#二佛法的修学次第}\n    81\t## II. The Stages of Practice
in the Dharma\n    82\t\n    83\tMost of the major schools of Chinese Buddhism t
ook shape during the Sui and Tang dynasties, when practitioners had sharper facu
lties and a firmer grounding in doctrine. For this reason, the founding masters 
did not focus on laying foundations but on expounding the perfect-and-sudden tea
chings, so that those of sharp faculties and keen wisdom could directly realize 
their own mind and attain buddhahood on the spot. Now we are in the degenerate a
ge of the Dharma; the faculties of sentient beings are shallow, and for most peo
ple the perfect-and-sudden methods are like clouds on the horizon---within sight
but beyond reach. Recognizing the common foundations of practice and building a 
sequence of practice accessible to all is thus the urgent task of the Buddhist c
ommunity.\n    84\t\n    85\tEarlier we spoke of the core elements of the Dharma
; in fact, discussing the stages of practice likewise cannot do without these. T
he content is the same, but the emphasis and meaning differ. In discussing the s
tages of practice, the key is to recognize the progressive, layer-upon-layer rel
ations among taking refuge, generating aspiration, observing precepts, establish
ing right view, and practicing samatha-vipassana.\n    86\t\n    87\tTo study Bu
ddhism, one must first take refuge. Taking refuge is the root of the Dharma: wit
hout taking refuge, or without taking it seriously, practice is like a tree with
out roots. Taking refuge is also the root of faith: whether we come to depend on
the Three Jewels, and whether we can accept, believe, and practice the teachings
of the Tripitaka, both depend on the depth of our refuge. It is because we take 
refuge in the Three Jewels that we can generate aspiration, observe precepts, he
ar and contemplate the scriptures, establish right view, and cultivate samatha-v
ipassana. Otherwise, none of this could even be spoken of.\n    88\t\n    89\tTh
en we must generate aspiration. Aspiration is the wish for the qualities with wh
ich the Three Jewels are endowed. Without this wish to emulate what is worthy, o
ne cannot be counted a true Buddhist. Because of this wish, life has a goal and 
practice has momentum. Therefore, after taking refuge, we must generate aspirati
on; only on this basis can we go on to cultivate precepts, right view, and samat
ha-vipassana. Aspiration is the root of practice: if we generate the wrong kind 
of aspiration, then whatever we do has nothing to do with practice.\n    90\t\n 
  91\tPrecepts stand at the head of the three trainings and are the foundation o
f concentration and wisdom---from precepts arises concentration, and based on co
ncentration, wisdom arises. Thus, after taking refuge and generating aspiration,
we must also receive and observe the precepts; this is the standard course of Bu
ddhist practice. According to the Buddha\'s regulations, a monk should "study th
e precepts for five years without leaving his teacher," and the Vinaya stipulate
s that one may not study the Abhidharma before learning the precepts. All this f
ully shows that observing the precepts is the necessary precondition for establi
shing right view and cultivating samatha-vipassana.\n    92\t\n    93\tRight vie
w and samatha-vipassana are the keys to putting the Dharma into practice. Right 
view is the eye of samatha-vipassana, while samatha-vipassana is the technique f
or bringing right view into mind and conduct; the two complement each other. Wit
hout the guidance of right view, the practice of samatha-vipassana often cannot 
find its way in; and knowledge not verified by samatha-vipassana easily falls in
to the trap of empty theorizing. Viewed in terms of the stages of practice, we s
hould first learn right view and then cultivate samatha-vipassana. The four prac
tices---associating with virtuous teachers, hearing the true Dharma, applying ap
propriate reflection, and practicing in accordance with the Dharma---also illust
rate this. Practicing on the basis of right view can also help us correct every 
deviation in our minds. Otherwise, taking refuge may lapse into superstition, as
piration may carry impurities, observing precepts may become a mere formality, a
nd samatha-vipassana may end in fruitless toil.\n    94\t\n    95\tAlthough the 
five links deepen in sequence, this does not mean that we only begin generating 
aspiration after completing refuge, nor that once we generate aspiration we need
no longer practice taking refuge. In fact, all five links can reach their ultima
te completion only with liberation or buddhahood. Thus, this sequence represents
only the emphasis of each stage of practice. Aspiration arises because of refuge
, but aspiration is also a deepening of refuge; so in the course of generating a
spiration we should continue to practice taking refuge, and the same holds for t
he others. In this way they interpenetrate. But the basic order must not be viol
ated; otherwise practice will likewise run into problems.\n    96\t\n    97\tSom
e people start directly with samatha-vipassana meditation, thinking that studyin
g the teachings is like counting the sands of the sea---a futile effort. But wit
hout the guidance of right view, what are they ultimately practicing? What are t
hey ultimately realizing? What are they ultimately resolving? It is like enterin
g a station without knowing where one wants to go and boarding at random a train
bound for some equally unknown destination. After riding for years, even decades
, they remain muddled, not knowing where they will end up.\n    98\t\n    99\tSo
me people start directly with studying the teachings, but having read many great
sutras and treatises, they often come to disregard norms such as the rules of di
scipline and conduct. And without the protection and guardianship of the precept
s, the knowledge gained through hearing and contemplation, built only at the lev
el of consciousness, has no power to subdue afflictions and habitual tendencies.
\n   100\t\n   101\tSome people start directly with observing precepts. This is 
certainly important, but without correct aspiration and a clear understanding of
the meaning of the precepts, observing them becomes extremely difficult. Without
renunciation, merely passively resisting the five desires and six sense objects 
carries a high degree of difficulty. For the ordinary mind is sticky---it adhere
s to whatever it touches---and only by combining inner and outer efforts can we 
strengthen our countermeasures. And without bodhicitta, observing the bodhisattv
a precepts is also beset with difficulties, because ordinary beings care about "
me" and "all that is mine." Without a compassion that extends from oneself to ot
hers, how can one benefit sentient beings without hesitation?\n   102\t\n   103\
tLikewise, without an unwavering mind of refuge in the Three Jewels, aspiration 
cannot be maintained for long. Where does the strength of renunciation and bodhi
citta come from? Precisely from our longing for and faith in the Three Jewels. B
ecause we long for liberation and believe we can accomplish liberation as the Bu
ddha did, we arouse renunciation and cut off attachment to self and dharmas; bec
ause we long for buddhahood and believe we can perfect the fruit of buddhahood a
s the Buddha did, we arouse bodhicitta and accomplish the supreme path.\n   104\
t\n   105\tUnderstanding the stages of practice can help us establish a step-by-
step path of learning Buddhism, clarify the emphasis of each stage, and understa
nd why they enhance one another. When practice reaches a certain stage, we can c
learly know what tasks this stage requires, what it adds to earlier practice, an
d what foundation it should lay for later practice. At the same time, it helps u
s examine and correct the various problems that arise from practicing without re
gard for the stages.\n   106\t\n   107\tAnd the first three links of the sequenc
e---taking refuge, generating aspiration, and observing precepts---are the commo
n foundation that no practice method can bypass. Views and samatha-vipassana are
more school-specific: views differ among Consciousness-only, Tiantai, and Madhya
maka, and the ways of applying the mind in meditation also differ. If views and 
samatha-vipassana are compared to specialized fields, then taking refuge, genera
ting aspiration, and observing precepts are the basic education one must receive
before choosing a specialty. With a solid foundation, one is able to go deep int
o a specialized field.\n   108\t\n   109\tThese words may sound like commonplace
s that need no special emphasis, but the reality before us gives little cause fo
r optimism. Looking at teaching in the Buddhist academies, monastic students stu
dy the doctrines of every school but cannot put them to use; some even see their
faith weaken and their aspiration for the path decline. Lay practitioners have s
imilar problems: though they have read many sutras and treatises, they do not ta
ke the foundations of practice seriously, thinking that taking refuge is too ele
mentary, aspiration lacks depth, and precepts are outdated. And what is the resu
lt? They speak with glib fluency but accomplish nothing in practice. If those wh
o study doctrine tend toward empty theorizing, what of those who emphasize actua
l practice? Whether reciting the Buddha\'s name or sitting in meditation, those 
who truly practice with results are equally rare. Most merely recite in a muddle
and sit in a daze. Empty theorizing and blind practice seem at first glance to b
e two unrelated extremes, but the crux of the problem is the same: they have ski
pped what should not be skipped and omitted what should not be omitted.\n   110\
t\n   111\t{#三五大要素的实践}\n   112\t## III. Putting the Five Elements into P
ractice\n   113\t\n   114\tSo how do we follow these core elements to complete t
he practice of the Buddha path? In other words, how do we put each element into 
practice? Below, I will briefly explain the emphasis and existing problems of ea
ch.\n   115\t\n   116\t{#皈-依}\n   117\t### 1. Taking Refuge\n   118\t\n   119\
tThe Dharma is boundless, yet it has but one entrance: taking refuge in the Thre
e Jewels. As the beginning of practice, how do we enter through this gate, deepe
n it continuously, and ultimately accomplish the qualities of the Three Jewels w
ithin ourselves?\n   120\t\n   121\tFirst, we examine our motivation. Is our mot
ivation for taking refuge correct? Are we taking refuge in the Three Jewels to b
e liberated from samsara, to attain buddhahood and become a patriarch? If we tak
e refuge merely to seek the blessings of human and celestial rebirth, peace and 
good fortune, we may receive the Buddhas\' protection, but we only form some goo
d affinities, which have nothing to do with practice or transforming our lives. 
In fact, this kind of faith often contains elements of superstition and blind fa
ith and cannot be counted as the faith of a true Buddhist disciple.\n   122\t\n 
 123\tSecond, we obtain the refuge commitment. At the time of taking refuge, are
we sincere? Do we have full confidence in the object of refuge? Do we yearn for 
the goal that refuge leads to? Do we vow from the depths of our hearts to take t
he Three Jewels as the refuge of body and mind, to solve the problems of life wi
th the Dharma, and to help others with it? If our aspiration at the time of taki
ng refuge is incorrect, or the ceremony is not conducted in accordance with the 
Dharma, we cannot obtain the refuge commitment.\n   124\t\n   125\tThird, we saf
eguard the refuge commitment. Obtaining the refuge commitment is not a once-and-
for-all matter; we must carefully guard it afterward. In particular, we must not
violate the vows made at the time of taking refuge: "I take refuge in the Buddha
and will never take refuge in devas, maras, or followers of other paths; I take 
refuge in the Dharma and will never take refuge in the scriptures of other paths
; I take refuge in the Sangha and will never take refuge in the misguided assemb
lies of other paths." If we transgress these, the refuge commitment is broken.\n
  126\t\n   127\tFourth, we practice and strengthen the refuge. Any thought or s
kill must be repeated and habituated over a long period to be strengthened, and 
refuge is no exception; it is by no means accomplished in a single ceremony. The
refore, after taking refuge, we must practice it daily and recall it constantly,
so as to strengthen the place of the Three Jewels in our minds. Otherwise, its w
eight will naturally dwindle, until it is replaced by other things. Since the be
ginning of this year, I have been vigorously promoting the practice of refuge in
various places and have compiled a group-practice liturgy centered on the practi
ce of refuge, covering aspiration, repentance, offerings, analytical meditation,
placement meditation, vows, and dedication of merit---suitable both as an indivi
dual\'s daily practice and as the regular religious life of monasteries leading 
the faithful in group practice.\n   128\t\n   129\tThe more we trust the Three J
ewels, the stronger our mind of refuge, and correspondingly the greater the effe
ct the Dharma has on us. Thus, whether practice can succeed and accomplish its i
ntended goals depends first of all on the quality of our refuge.\n   130\t\n   1
31\t{#发-心}\n   132\t### 2. Generating Aspiration\n   133\t\n   134\tWhether we
study Buddhism or not, whatever we do, we cannot do without aspiration; the only
difference is what kind of aspiration we generate. Since beginningless time, wha
t we have generated is the ordinary mind---greed, hatred, and ignorance---and th
is is what has produced our present ordinary character. When we recognize the ha
rm the ordinary mind has brought to our lives and no longer wish to sink into su
ch a life, we must counter it with correct aspiration.\n   135\t\n   136\tThere 
are two kinds of correct aspiration: one is renunciation, the other bodhicitta. 
Whatever aspiration we practice with, that is what we will ultimately accomplish
. Moreover, if deviations or impurities creep into the process of generating asp
iration, a slight error at the start will lead a thousand miles astray---this ca
lls for particular attention. Aspiration represents a strong wish in life, but w
hen first generated it is only a single thought; it must be continually strength
ened through analytical meditation before a spark can start a prairie fire.\n   
137\t\n   138\tPractitioners of the sravaka vehicle seek liberation; they see th
e world as a prison and the three realms as a burning house, unwilling to stay a
moment longer---this is renunciation. In fact, if renunciation is genuine and in
tense, liberation is already half accomplished. Why is practice so difficult for
modern people? The key lies in renunciation not being earnest. Renunciation is r
enunciation of the five desires and six sense objects, renunciation of ignorance
and afflictions, and above all renunciation of the delusion and karma of birth a
nd death. If our wish to renounce is strong and firm, we will no longer cling to
anything in the world. The afflictions built upon attachment will then loosen an
d crumble away.\n   139\t\n   140\tTherefore, we should often observe and contem
plate the suffering of samsara and see clearly that we are trapped in the three 
realms, unable to be free. Then we will be like a prisoner eagerly longing for f
reedom: given the chance to leave, we would not want to stay one minute longer i
n the cage. In fact, this is exactly our situation in samsara. Once we realize t
his, there is nothing to cling to in wealth, lust, fame, food, or sleep, because
clinging can only mean staying in prison.\n   141\t\n   142\tAs practitioners of
the bodhisattva path, we must also arouse renunciation. Not only that---out of c
ompassion for sentient beings, bodhisattvas see them turning endlessly in the se
a of suffering and cannot bear to leave alone; they vow to help all beings gain 
liberation together. This compassion that extends from oneself to others is bodh
icitta, the root of accomplishing the Buddha\'s qualities. As the Chapter on Sam
antabhadra\'s Practices and Vows states: "The Buddhas and Tathagatas take great 
compassion as their very essence. Because of sentient beings, great compassion a
rises; because of great compassion, bodhicitta arises; because of bodhicitta, th
ey attain perfect enlightenment."\n   143\t\n   144\tThe arising and strengtheni
ng of bodhicitta must likewise be supported by analytical meditation and placeme
nt meditation. The Venerable Atisha\'s seven causes and effects, Shantideva\'s e
xchanging self and other, and Master Sheng\'an\'s Exhortation to Arouse the Bodh
i Mind are all sublime instructions for deepening bodhicitta. Apart from these p
sychological guides, we must also receive and practice a bodhicitta liturgy, rec
iting and contemplating it three times each day and night, constantly reminding 
ourselves: What am I to accomplish? What is my goal in life? This is not just sh
outing empty slogans---that is of no use. The key is to wish for it from the dep
ths of the heart and to vow with genuine sincerity.\n   145\t\n   146\t{#戒-律}\
n   147\t### 3. Observing Precepts\n   148\t\n   149\tPrecepts are the root that
accords with liberation and also the root of supreme enlightenment. Through the 
behavioral norms of "this should be done, this should not be done," they provide
a wholesome mental environment for practice, helping us reach smoothly the goals
of liberation and buddhahood.\n   150\t\n   151\tCorresponding to the practice o
f the path of liberation and the bodhisattva path, precepts are likewise divided
into the precepts of individual liberation and the bodhisattva precepts. The pre
cepts of individual liberation include the five precepts and eight precepts for 
laypeople, and the novice precepts and monks\' precepts for monastics---the stan
dards of conduct for practitioners on the path of liberation. Their emphasis is 
on restraint: by keeping away from the five desires and worldly toils and discip
lining our behavior, we stop the continuation of the deluded stream, subdue affl
ictions, and move toward liberation.\n   152\t\n   153\tThe bodhisattva precepts
, on top of the precepts of restraint, add the precepts of performance, so that 
ceasing evil and doing good go forward together. The myriad practices of the six
perfections advocated by the bodhisattva precepts are both the provisions for at
taining supreme enlightenment and sharp weapons for dismantling the ordinary min
d: giving overcomes stinginess and greed, observing precepts counteracts neglige
nce, and patience conquers anger. At the same time, these virtuous deeds are the
standard for examining whether bodhicitta has been accomplished. When we practic
e giving, can we give up everything we own without reserve? Do we make no distin
ction of closeness or distance toward those who receive? Have we ceased clinging
to a self who gives and an other who receives?\n   154\t\n   155\tThe bodhisattv
a precepts have two major systems: the Brahma Net and the Yogacara. By compariso
n, the allowances and prohibitions of the Yogacara Bodhisattva Precepts are more
skillful, which is why Master Taixu of the modern era vigorously advocated them.
The Brahma Net Bodhisattva Precepts, on the other hand, represent the level of g
reat bodhisattvas and are quite difficult for ordinary beings to uphold. In the 
Chinese Buddhist community, the bodhisattva precepts are included as one of the 
three platforms of the triple-platform ordination, so the vast majority of monas
tics have received them; even among laypeople, a considerable number have receiv
ed the bodhisattva precepts. This should be a gratifying phenomenon, yet what is
worrying is that, although so many people have received the bodhisattva precepts
, they often do not know what bodhicitta is and have never developed the habit o
f cultivating compassion---they are "bodhisattvas" in name only, a mere formalit
y.\n   156\t\n   157\tIn view of this, I have compiled a liturgy for receiving a
nd practicing bodhicitta, drawing on the traditions of bodhicitta practice in th
e two great systems of Madhyamaka and Yogacara and the bodhicitta instructions o
f the Lamrim, and I have begun transmitting it to the students of the Jiechuang 
Institute of Buddhist Studies. Bodhicitta is the soul of the bodhisattva precept
s; therefore, before receiving the bodhisattva precepts, one should train by aro
using bodhicitta and earnestly practicing compassion, and only after laying the 
corresponding foundation receive the bodhisattva precepts. Only in this way can 
we truly carry out the spirit of the bodhisattva path and become true children o
f the Buddha who shoulder the Tathagata\'s great work.\n   158\t\n   159\t{#正-
见}\n   160\t### 4. Establishing Right View\n   161\t\n   162\tRight view heads 
the Noble Eightfold Path; it is the compass of all practice methods and the ligh
thouse of the long path of cultivation. Because aspiration cannot be accomplishe
d in one step, precepts cannot be perfected overnight, and even the deepening of
refuge is not achieved at a stroke, right view plays a decisive role in this pro
cess, guiding us to correct deviations of mind and weed out its impurities.\n   
163\t\n   164\tFor practice on the path of liberation, the right views of depend
ent origination and karma and its results are extremely important. Life is the a
ccumulation of habits: what we do and think determines what our future life will
be. Thus, in the world of dependent origination, karma is everything. Moreover, 
the views of impermanence, no-self, and emptiness are basic cognitions that prac
tice must possess. Practice on the bodhisattva path is likewise inseparable from
the guidance of right view; only so can conventional bodhicitta be elevated into
ultimate bodhicitta. Ordinary beings cling to the marks of self, person, sentien
t being, and life span---like a limited vessel that confines the mind within it.
Only the view of emptiness can shatter this confinement, returning the finite to
the infinite, so that we can benefit all sentient beings with an impartial, self
less mind and complete the provisions for the fruit of buddhahood.\n   165\t\n  
166\tSo how can we obtain and consolidate right view? It cannot be separated fro
m associating with virtuous teachers and hearing and contemplating the scripture
s. Based on many years of teaching experience, I believe this learning process s
hould go deep into a single approach. Modern people tend to study in a scattered
way, dabbling in Tiantai, Huayan, Madhyamaka, and Consciousness-only alike. Unle
ss one has extraordinary energy and sharp faculties, such study mostly skims the
surface: one seems to know a little of everything but is merely a "knowing intel
lectual," without even systematic knowledge, let alone right view. I think most 
people, in a lifetime of practice, need only the right view of a single school--
-whether the view of impermanence or no-self, or the Consciousness-only view or 
the view of emptiness. Practice one weapon until it is fully mastered and wielde
d with ease; you can still defeat the enemy and press forward bravely.\n   167\t
\n   168\tBut the right view of hearing and contemplation, established at the le
vel of consciousness, is often very coarse, because human thinking has many pitf
alls. How can it be made precise? One way is through debate and mutual refinemen
t among teachers and peers, exposing the blind spots and problems in our underst
anding, discarding the dross, and keeping the essence to raise its purity. Anoth
er is through gaining experiential realization in meditation, because the essenc
e of the Dharma cannot be fully conveyed in words; those ineffable parts can onl
y be finally known through personal realization---as the saying goes, the person
who drinks the water knows best whether it is hot or cold.\n   169\t\n   170\t{#
止-观}\n   171\t### 5. Practicing Samatha-Vipassana\n   172\t\n   173\tSamatha, 
in Sanskrit, is another name for meditative concentration; it is also called sam
adhi. It includes samatha with an object and samatha without an object. The medi
tation of the Lesser Vehicle leans toward samatha with an object---that is, with
an object of focus. The practice of the Chan school and of the Tibetan tradition
s of Dzogchen and Mahamudra, by contrast, belongs to samatha without an object: 
through specific methods, one directly realizes emptiness and settles the mind i
n it. Vipassana, rendered in Chinese as insight, encompasses the levels of conte
mplation, visualization, and analytical meditation.\n   174\t\n   175\tMany peop
le misread the Chan school and one-sidedly reduce the practice of samatha-vipass
ana to non-discrimination. I think this is the main cause of the opposition betw
een Chan and the doctrinal schools. As a result, neglecting the scriptures, they
lack the right view gained through hearing and contemplation, and without a virt
uous teacher to guide them by the hand, how many Chan practitioners have fallen 
into blind practice!\n   176\t\n   177\tNon-discriminative meditation is the cul
tivation of non-cultivation---the meditation in which non-discriminative wisdom 
realizes emptiness. It is beyond the grasp of ordinary meditators, because our p
resent basis of mind is extremely unstable and built upon inverted views. If we 
start directly with non-discrimination, how can we adjust our wrong views and in
verted mental states? Therefore, we must take contemplation with discrimination 
as the precondition, freeing the mind from its inverted state, before we can ent
er non-discriminative meditation.\n   178\t\n   179\tAt the same time, the pract
ice of samatha is indispensable. Although samatha is a practice common to Buddhi
sm and non-Buddhist paths, it is an important foundation for cultivating insight
. In Chinese Buddhism, the theory of insight is extremely rich---so why can so f
ew people actually put it into practice today? Precisely because samatha has bee
n neglected for so long. If we can establish the foundation of samatha and then 
use right contemplation to break through attachment to self and dharmas, realizi
ng emptiness is not as far out of reach as we imagine.\n   180\t\n   181\tApart 
from the engagement of mind free from discrimination and fabrication spoken of i
n the Chan school, contemplation with discrimination is also indispensable: anal
ytical meditation and visualization meditation are both excellent ways of practi
ce. What is good about them is that even ordinary people without a foundation in
meditative concentration can practice them. It can be said that anyone who can t
hink normally can do analytical meditation, and anyone with imagination can do v
isualization meditation. Of course, although analytical and visualization medita
tion do not demand much of practitioners, they must still be based on the corres
ponding right view gained through hearing and contemplation. Only then, through 
appropriate reflection or visualization, can we abide in mindfulness and clear a
wareness. For those who study doctrine, making good use of analytical meditation
---adjusting mind and conduct by reflecting on the meaning of the Dharma they ha
ve learned---is an excellent way to combine study with practice.\n   182\t\n   1
83\t{#四结-说}\n   184\t## IV. Conclusion\n   185\t\n   186\tIn summary, taking 
refuge, generating aspiration, observing precepts, establishing right view, and 
practicing samatha-vipassana are not only the core elements of Buddhist study an
d practice but also the stages of practice that run through it from beginning to
end. Grasping these five elements, we grasp the key stages from beginning to stu
dy Buddhism to attaining buddhahood. At the same time, they are like the great e
arth that nurtures all things---the common foundation on which all practice meth
ods are established. If we take them seriously and truly put them into practice 
in mind and conduct, then whether our starting point is high or low, whether our
faculties are sharp or dull, whether our progress is fast or slow, we are all ad
vancing toward the destination step by step. This is not only of great significa
nce for individual practice; it is also a strong guarantee of the healthy develo
pment of Chinese Buddhism in today\'s society.\n   187\t\n   188\t# Reflections 
on Chinese Buddhism\n   189\t\n   190\tPublished during the Spring Festival of 2
004\n   191\t\n   192\tIn the history of Chinese culture, although there were th
e Hundred Schools of Thought in the pre-Qin era, the three traditions active in 
society since the Han and Wei dynasties have mainly been Confucianism, Buddhism,
and Daoism. Among them, only Buddhism is a foreign culture. Yet in its two thous
and and more years of circulation in China, it has become intimately blended wit
h the traditional culture, deeply influencing the Chinese people with its rich t
hought and doctrine, standing as one of three pillars alongside the native Confu
cian and Daoist traditions and becoming an important part of Chinese culture. Co
rrectly understanding Buddhism can not only help us recognize the truth of life 
and resolve afflictions and suffering, but also give us a comprehensive, in-dept
h understanding of traditional Chinese culture.\\\n   193\tBuddhism entered Chin
a during the reign of Emperor Ai of the Western Han, spread through the Northern
and Southern Dynasties, and reached its peak in the Sui and Tang. In the early p
eriod of its spread in China, Buddhism was mainly engaged in two kinds of work: 
the translation of sutras and doctrinal studies. The translation of sutras enabl
ed Chinese Buddhism to inherit the Buddhist thought of early and middle-period I
ndia fairly completely, while doctrinal studies led Buddhism in China to form di
fferent systems of thought---namely the eight major schools of Tiantai, Three Tr
eatise, Huayan, Chan, Pure Land, Consciousness-only, Vinaya, and Esoteric.\\\n  
194\tThe formation of schools is not a new phenomenon unique to China. Different
sects appeared in early India as well as in the Theravada and Tibetan regions. T
he reason schools arose is mainly that Buddhism is so vast and profound that no 
one can master it completely. In order to spread the Dharma and to make practice
easier for people, the patriarchs built their own systems of study and practice 
on the basis of a particular sutra or treatise, or a group of similar ones, whic
h then evolved into different schools.\\\n   195\tThe major schools of Chinese B
uddhism were mostly founded in the Sui and Tang, but their histories can be trac
ed back to the Northern and Southern Dynasties or even earlier. For example, the
founding patriarch of the Pure Land school was Huiyuan of the Eastern Jin, while
its actual founder was Shandao of the Tang. The founding patriarch of the Tianta
i school was Huiwen of the Northern Qi, who, on reading the Mahaprajnaparamita S
astra, realized "the three wisdoms attained in a single mind," and from the Madh
yamaka Sastra awakened to "three insights in one mind." Huisi inherited this tea
ching and, combined with the essentials of the Lotus Sutra, transmitted it to Zh
iyi, who formed the ideas of "one thought containing the three thousand realms" 
and "the perfect interfusion of the three truths" and formally established the T
iantai school. From this it is clear that no system of thought is accomplished o
vernight; it must accumulate over several hundred years and be summed up by seve
ral generations before it takes shape. Afterward, it still depends on being pass
ed down from generation to generation by those accomplished in practice, to keep
it from falling.\\\n   196\tChinese sectarian Buddhism has its own characteristi
cs. First, each school has its own foundational scriptures: the Tiantai school r
elies on the Lotus Sutra, and the Huayan school on the Avatamsaka Sutra. Second,
each school has its own intellectual lineage, either originating in India---as t
he Consciousness-only school takes Maitreya Bodhisattva as its founding patriarc
h and the Three Treatise school takes Nagarjuna---or sprouting on native soil, l
ike the Pure Land and Tiantai schools mentioned above. Third, each school has di
fferent criteria for evaluating the Buddha\'s entire teaching---that is, doctrin
al classification, such as Tiantai\'s five periods and eight teachings and Huaya
n\'s five teachings. Beyond this, because of differences in each school\'s core 
theory, each also formed its own distinctive practice methods.\\\n   197\tThe fo
rmation of Chinese sectarian Buddhism not only marked the maturity of the Chines
e people\'s thinking about and understanding of the Dharma, but also embodied th
e completion of the localization of Chinese Buddhism. Sectarian Buddhism inherit
ed the thought of Indian Mahayana Buddhism, but this inheritance was not a copy;
it took root in the soil of Chinese civilization and absorbed the color of nativ
e culture. The prevalence of Tiantai, Huayan, and Chan, for instance, was relate
d to traditional Chinese culture and the temperament of the Chinese people. It c
an be said that sectarian Buddhism is Buddhism with Chinese characteristics.\\\n
  198\tFrom the Tang and Song onward, as China\'s national power declined, the s
pread of Buddhism also went into decline. Among the schools, the Consciousness-o
nly school, lacking a foundation in Chinese culture, faded into oblivion after o
nly two or three generations despite the exhausting efforts of the Tripitaka Mas
ter Xuanzang. The Vinaya and Three Treatise schools likewise failed to spread wi
dely in China; after the Buddhist persecutions under Emperor Wuzong and Emperor 
Shizong, their important texts were scattered and lost. Fortunately, these lost 
texts were still preserved in neighboring Japan and returned to their homeland i
n the late Qing and early Republic. As for Tiantai and Huayan, although revered 
by the Chinese, their transmissions of realization were not carried down intact,
and in the end there was doctrine without contemplation. In the final analysis, 
only Chan and Pure Land became widespread because their theory and practice were
simple---yet, in reality, they too had no small number of problems.\\\n   199\tT
he decline of these methods filled me with regret and also impelled me to keep r
eflecting: where does the cause of the decline truly lie? Over many years of stu
dy and practice, I gradually discovered that some of these schools do indeed hav
e innate deficiencies: they can serve as a system of thought or a method of prac
tical cultivation, but are not enough to form an independent system of study and
practice. I believe that a complete system of study and practice should have fiv
e elements: taking refuge, bodhicitta, precepts, the right view gained through h
earing and contemplation, and the practice of samatha-vipassana---none of them c
an be missing.\\\n   200\tTaking refuge is the basic procedure for becoming a Bu
ddhist disciple and the necessary precondition for accepting, believing, and pra
cticing the Buddha\'s teachings. Whether Buddhist disciples can earnestly practi
ce the Buddha\'s Dharma depends on whether they have generated a genuine mind of
refuge in the Three Jewels and truly recognize the role of the Three Jewels in i
mproving life and realizing life\'s highest value. Even with such recognition, a
fter taking refuge one must continue to practice taking refuge, because life is 
an endless accumulation, and in the course of it the ordinary mind has long sinc
e formed tremendous force. Only by continually contemplating death and impermane
nce and visualizing the qualities of the Three Jewels can we reduce the workings
of the ordinary mind, let the Three Jewels gradually take root and sprout within
us, and eventually make them the dominant force of our lives. Unfortunately, the
Chinese schools have mostly not especially promoted the practice of taking refug
e, so that the Buddhist community today shows a serious weakening of faith.\\\n 
 201\tThe Mahayana is by nature a Buddhism of active engagement with the world, 
yet in China it has left people with the impression of passive renunciation. Why
is this? In the final analysis, it is because bodhicitta has been neglected. In 
the past, we always assumed that Mahayana scriptures represented Mahayana Buddhi
sm and that studying Mahayana sutras and treatises naturally made one a Mahayana
practitioner. We failed to realize that the key distinguishing the Mahayana from
the lesser vehicles lies in aspiration---in whether bodhicitta is present. For b
odhicitta is the distinctive cause of buddhahood and the distinctive teaching of
the Mahayana. Looking back at the major schools of Chinese Buddhism, they have g
enerally not attached enough importance to bodhicitta. It is for this very reaso
n that, in many people\'s minds, bodhisattva practices such as the six perfectio
ns and four means of embracing seem to be taught only for great bodhisattvas, wi
th no connection to one\'s own practice.\\\n   202\tPrecepts are the foundation 
of Buddhist practice. The sravaka vehicle\'s fivefold dharmakaya consists of pre
cepts, concentration, wisdom, liberation, and the knowledge and vision of libera
tion, and the Mahayana likewise regards precepts as the root of supreme enlighte
nment---all of which shows the importance of precepts in practice. When the Chin
ese patriarchs constructed the precepts into a school of their own, their intent
ion must have been to strengthen people\'s respect for the precepts, so that the
y would study and observe them better. But when the precepts exist as an indepen
dent school, some people tend to inflate their role without limit, thinking that
observing the precepts is all of practice. In fact, the precepts cannot serve as
a complete system of study and practice, because they are only the common founda
tion of practice, not everything. At the same time, studying the precepts is not
the exclusive preserve of Vinaya practitioners but the code of conduct for every
Buddhist disciple.\\\n   203\tThe right view gained through hearing and contempl
ation is also very important. The Noble Eightfold Path is the essential practice
of Buddhism, and right view heads it. The four factors of stream entry in the Ag
amas are associating with virtuous teachers, hearing the true Dharma, applying a
ppropriate reflection, and practicing in accordance with the Dharma; in the Maha
yana sutras and treatises, it is advocated to enter samadhi through hearing, con
templation, and practice. It is clear that both the greater and lesser vehicles\
' scriptures attach great importance to obtaining right view. Only under the gui
dance of right view can one practice in accordance with the Dharma. Yet in Chine
se Buddhism, the most widely spread schools---Chan and Pure Land---have not part
icularly emphasized the importance of the right view gained through hearing and 
contemplation. When Bodhidharma came from the West, along with transmitting the 
Buddha\'s mind seal, he also proposed using the Lankavatara Sutra to seal the mi
nd---but it was used only to verify the mind after enlightenment. And the Chan s
logan---"not established on words and letters, a special transmission outside th
e scriptures, pointing directly to the mind, seeing one\'s nature and attaining 
buddhahood"---has become the basis on which many people distance themselves from
the scriptures. Of course, in the Sui and Tang era, when doctrinal study was hig
hly developed, many people already had a deep doctrinal foundation before enteri
ng the Chan gate, so not emphasizing right view seemed to do no great harm. More
over, at that time eminent masters appeared in abundance: even if practitioners 
were weaker in doctrine, the earnest instruction of great virtuous teachers kept
their practice from going astray. But in an age when doctrine has declined and t
he Chan school has lost its masters, if one still does not emphasize the right v
iew gained through hearing and contemplation, practice is nothing short of blind
cultivation.\\\n   204\tTeaching and contemplation are the two great essentials 
of Buddhist practice. Any school must have not only scriptures as its theoretica
l construction but also the practical methods of yogic samatha-vipassana. The pr
actice methods of each school were mostly passed down from generation to generat
ion by the patriarchs, who inherited the Buddha\'s teachings and combined them w
ith their own practice experience. The Three Treatise school had Kumarajiva, Sen
gzhao, Senglang, Sengquan, Falang, and Jizang; the Huayan school had Dushun, Zhi
yan, Fazang, Chengguan, and Zongmi; and the Chan school, still more, saw one flo
wer bloom into five petals, keeping the dharma lineage alive. These schools all 
had very sublime transmissions of realization in their early days, but today mos
t of them have become blurred. Because the transmission of practice and realizat
ion has been weak, many practitioners cannot bring the right view gained through
hearing and contemplation into the practice of samatha-vipassana, making it a me
ans of arousing fundamental wisdom and realizing emptiness. At the same time, la
cking experience of the wisdom of emptiness, they cannot go deep in their study 
of doctrine either.\\\n   205\tSince the late Qing and early Republic, Buddhism 
has shown a trend of revival. At that time, the Venerable Xuyun promoted Chan, M
aster Yinguang promoted the Pure Land, Venerables Dixian and Tanxu promoted Tian
tai, Venerable Xingci promoted Huayan, and Master Hongyi, a Vinaya master, promo
ted the Vinaya school. These great masters taught in their own regions across th
e country and had a certain influence on society. While they were promoting the 
traditional sectarian Buddhism, Master Taixu was energetically devoted to promot
ing Humanistic Buddhism---on the one hand, to reform the bad practices that had 
accumulated in the spread of Buddhism, and on the other, to advocate that Buddhi
sm should stand on real human life and reach the Buddha path from the human vehi
cle, in the words of the saying, "when one perfects being human, one attains bud
dhahood."\\\n   206\tThe proposal of Humanistic Buddhism gave a new image to a B
uddhism that had drifted into mystification, spirit worship, and otherworldlines
s. It enabled Buddhism to walk into real human life and give comprehensive atten
tion to the various problems existing in society, fully embodying Buddhism\'s co
mpassionate spirit of benefiting the world and helping others---undoubtedly of g
reat significance for the popularization of Buddhism. But some people fail to gr
asp the true content of Humanistic Buddhism, thinking that Buddhism cares only a
bout happiness in real human life and forgetting Buddhism\'s distinctive three-v
ehicle liberation and the distinctive teachings of the Mahayana. This has caused
the spread of Buddhism in today\'s society to show a tendency toward superficial
ity and secularization, a phenomenon worthy of our attention.\\\n   207\tIn the 
contemporary spread of Buddhism, there has also appeared a trend different from 
the past: the academization of Buddhism. For a considerable period, Buddhism was
dismissed by the world as superstition. It was only after Elder Zhao Puchu put f
orward the saying "Buddhism is culture" that Buddhism once again drew the attent
ion of the cultural world. In recent years, many universities have also begun to
value Buddhist studies, setting up institutes of religious studies and Buddhist 
studies centers one after another, and quite a few scholars have thrown themselv
es into the wave of Buddhist research. The Buddhist community has responded quit
e actively as well: some young monks have entered domestic and foreign universit
ies to pursue degrees, and temples everywhere have been holding academic confere
nces and founding academic journals. This trend is clearly of positive significa
nce for promoting Buddhism in the cultural world. But what scholarship can atten
d to is only the cultural dimension of Buddhism; the profound doctrines and inne
r realization of Buddhism cannot be touched merely through academic research. If
the sangha, which upholds the Dharma and shoulders the Tathagata\'s work, cares 
only about the academic dimension of Buddhism, it is bound to lose the sangha\'s
true character.\\\n   208\tIn view of the various ills that have appeared in the
course of Buddhism\'s spread, I believe that the future development of Buddhism 
should pay attention to the following aspects. First, we should attach importanc
e to the practice of taking refuge---this is where the root of the Dharma lies; 
without valuing refuge, the root of the Dharma is lost. Second is bodhicitta: th
e study and practice of Mahayana methods must take bodhicitta as its preconditio
n; only in this way can one be led onto the Buddha path, rather than remaining i
n the human and celestial vehicle or seeking only one\'s own liberation. Third i
s observing the precepts: precepts are the foundation of all practice and the ba
sic guarantee of accomplishment in practice. Fourth is the right view gained thr
ough hearing and contemplation: with right view as the guide, practice can have 
the correct direction, instead of frittering away one\'s whole life in groping a
bout. Fifth is the practice of samatha-vipassana, by which the wisdom of the Dha
rma is put into practice in the mind and becomes a force for improving life. Lac
king this core, the doctrines one has studied will lose their vitality.\\\n   20
9\tPerhaps someone will say, "The mind itself is the Buddha" or "one\'s own natu
re is Amitabha; the mind alone is the Pure Land"---why must it be so complicated
? Indeed, these two phrases represent a very high view. But as views of an extre
mely high level, they are not suitable for everyone. In fact, their starting poi
nt is so high that only a very few of sharp faculties and keen wisdom are capabl
e of shouldering them. For "the mind itself is the Buddha" is not the present st
ate of ordinary people: although sentient beings possess the Buddha-nature, they
have remained in the state of the ordinary mind since beginningless time. And to
go from the ordinary mind to the perfect accomplishment of the Buddha mind, ther
e must be skillful methods as a path. Otherwise, even if we shout "the mind itse
lf is the Buddha" or "one\'s own nature is Amitabha; the mind alone is the Pure 
Land" for ten thousand years, we still cannot be liberated from the state of the
ordinary mind.\\\n   210\tSo what is the key to abandoning the ordinary mind and
accomplishing the Buddha mind? It is bodhicitta, the bodhisattva practice, and r
ight view. The vows and actions of ordinary beings both start from the ordinary 
mind, so what they finally accomplish is naturally the ordinary mind. If we wish
to accomplish supreme enlightenment, we must arouse bodhicitta. And the six perf
ections and four means of embracing of the bodhisattva path are indispensable sk
illful means for accomplishing the two virtues of compassion and wisdom of the f
ruit of buddhahood. Although sentient beings originally possess the Tathagata\'s
wisdom and virtuous marks, this treasure can only be unearthed with the appropri
ate skillful means. The noble ones of the sravaka vehicle, although they also re
alize the nature of reality, fail to accomplish immeasurable merits precisely be
cause they lack bodhicitta and the skillful practices of the six perfections and
four means of embracing. Besides bodhicitta and bodhisattva practice, right view
is also needed as guidance. Our present basis of mind is the ordinary mind, whic
h has long since become the master of our lives; whatever we do, it is difficult
to transcend its workings. The right view gained through hearing and contemplati
on can help us recognize the nature of the ordinary mind and fundamentally overc
ome and dismantle it, so that life can transcend it.\\\n   211\tFor the promotio
n of Humanistic Buddhism, bodhicitta and the view of emptiness are likewise indi
spensable. A single virtuous deed is hard to classify as belonging to which leve
l of Buddhist practice. Take the practice of giving: it is advocated by the huma
n and celestial vehicle, the path of liberation, and the bodhisattva path alike.
Practiced with the upward-striving mind of the human and celestial vehicle, it i
s a wholesome dharma of that vehicle; practiced with the renunciation of the sra
vaka vehicle, it is the practice of liberation; practiced with bodhicitta, it is
the bodhisattva practice. The bodhisattva practice includes the defiled practice
of ordinary beings and the undefiled practice of sages: bodhisattvas who have no
t yet reached the grounds have not escaped the workings of the ordinary mind, so
the giving they practice is still defiled virtuous action. But in the course of 
practicing giving, a bodhisattva continually uses the view of emptiness to elimi
nate the workings of the ordinary mind, and ultimately realizes emptiness and ac
complishes ultimate bodhicitta. From this it is clear that only under the guidan
ce of bodhicitta and the view of emptiness can the virtuous actions of the human
and celestial vehicles rise to the bodhisattva practice. Otherwise, Humanistic B
uddhism is very likely to evolve into human-vehicle Buddhism.\\\n   212\tI have 
put forward the five elements for studying and practicing Mahayana Buddhism. Alt
hough there is nothing new in them, they are the fundamental conditions that the
study and practice of every school must possess. I hope we can use them to re-ex
amine Chinese Buddhism, to see whether the schools we revere possess these condi
tions---or perhaps originally possessed them but have long since neglected them.
At the same time, we should examine our own practice: have we generated a genuin
e mind of refuge in the Three Jewels? Has bodhicitta been aroused? Do we live ac
cording to the precepts? Do we possess the right view gained through hearing and
contemplation? Can we put it into practice in samatha-vipassana, making it a mea
ns of realizing emptiness? Do we follow the corresponding stages of practice? If
these conditions are all present, I believe everyone\'s practice will surely bea
r fruit. If some link is missing, it should be made up in time. Then how is it t
o be made up? If one\'s own school originally has these contents, supplement fro
m one\'s own school; if one\'s own school lacks them, one should absorb the stre
ngths of other schools to perfect one\'s own school\'s study and practice.\n   2
13\t\n   214\tHere, let us together pray for the blessings of the Three Jewels a
nd the protection of the dragons and devas, and pray that our colleagues in the 
Buddhist community will join in generating aspiration, study and practice Chines
e sectarian Buddhism in accordance with these five elements, and absorb the stre
ngths of Tibetan and Theravada Buddhism. If these things can be done, I believe 
Chinese Buddhism will surely flourish and spread far.\n',
        message='214 lines read from file starting from line 1. Total lines in f
ile: 214. End of file reached.',
        display=[],
        extras=None
    )
)
