    text=' umaI have completed a thorough review of the translation of Master Ji
qun\'s essay, "Reflections on the Stages of Buddhist Practice."\n\n### **Overall
Assessment**\nThe translation is of exceptionally high quality. It captures the 
scholarly yet accessible tone of the original text and demonstrates a deep under
standing of Buddhist terminology. The mapping between the source and target is p
recise, and the English flow is natural and professional.\n\n---\n\n### **Detail
ed Findings**\n\n#### **Critical Issues**\n*None identified.*\n\n#### **Major Is
sues**\n*None identified.*\n\n#### **Minor Issues / Suggestions for Refinement**
\n\n| Source Line | Issue | Suggested Fix |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **L33** |
"those who truly grasp it all are one in a hundred" (真正对之了然于胸者百无一人)
| The Chinese implies "not a single person in a hundred." The English is close b
ut could be sharper: **"not one in a hundred truly grasps it."** |\n| **L43** | 
"mission we Buddhists of this generation cannot shirk" (我们这代佛子责无旁贷的使
命) | "Shirk" is correct, but **"duty... that we Buddhists of this generation ca
nnot evade"** or **"inevitable mission"** might feel more natural in a formal Bu
ddhist context. |\n| **L127** | "do not violate the vows... I take refuge in the
Buddha and will never take refuge in devas, maras, or followers of other paths" 
| The translation is accurate. However, in English Buddhist texts, "devas, maras
, or followers of other paths" (天魔外道) is often rendered as **"heavenly being
s, demons, or non-Buddhist paths"** for clarity to a wider audience. |\n| **L166
** | "the degenerate age of the Dharma" (末法) | This is a standard rendering. T
o be more precise in English academic Buddhist texts, **"the Dharma-Ending Age"*
* is often used. |\n| **L189** | "the eye of samatha-vipassana" (止观的眼目) | W
hile "eye" is a literal translation of 眼目, **"guiding light"** or **"essential
guide"** would be more idiomatic in English to convey the sense of "crucial guid
ing principle." |\n| **L197** | "the first three links... are the common foundat
ion" (前三个环节... 共同基础) | "Links" is acceptable, but **"stages"** or **"co
mponents"** is more consistent with the "Stages of Practice" (修学次第) theme of
the essay. |\n| **L222** | "speak with glib fluency" (说起来头头是道) | "Glib fl
uency" is a good translation, but **"speak with apparent authority"** or **"soun
d convincing in theory"** might better capture the nuance of someone who knows t
he jargon but lacks the practice. |\n\n---\n\n### **Terminology Consistency Chec
k**\nThe following key terms were used consistently throughout the text:\n*   **
皈依** $\\rightarrow$ Taking Refuge (Consistent)\n*   **发心** $\\rightarrow$ Ge
nerating Aspiration (Consistent)\n*   **戒律** $\\rightarrow$ Observing Precepts
(Consistent)\n*   **正见** $\\rightarrow$ Establishing Right View (Consistent)\n
*   **止观** $\\rightarrow$ Practicing Samatha-Vipassana (Consistent)\n*   **菩
提心** $\\rightarrow$ Bodhicitta (Consistent)\n*   **出离心** $\\rightarrow$ Ren
unciation (Consistent)\n*   **凡夫心** $\\rightarrow$ Ordinary Mind (Consistent)
\n*   **修学次第** $\\rightarrow$ Stages of Practice (Consistent)\n\n**Verdict: 
High-quality translation; recommended for approval with only minor stylistic pol
ish.**'
